{
  "results": [
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6900",
        "text": "Toucans are vibrant tropical birds found in the rainforests of Central and South America. Known for their oversized, colorful bills, they belong to the family Ramphastidae and are closely related to woodpeckers. There are over 40 species of toucans in the world."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6900"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6916",
        "text": "Toucans have small, round eyes surrounded by bright patches of bare skin. Their keen eyesight helps them spot ripe fruit in dense forest canopies. The eye ring can be blue, green, or red depending on the species."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6916"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6924",
        "text": "Toucan feathers are predominantly black with splashes of white, yellow, red, and orange. The colorful plumage serves as camouflage in the dappled light of the rainforest and plays a role in attracting mates during breeding season."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6924"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6933",
        "text": "The toucan's bill can be up to half the length of its body — up to 20 cm long. Despite its size, the bill is lightweight, made of keratin with a honeycomb bone structure inside. It is used to reach fruit, regulate body temperature, and deter predators."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6933"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6944",
        "text": "Toucans have zygodactyl feet — two toes pointing forward and two backward — giving them a strong grip on branches. Their sharp claws allow them to perch securely in the forest canopy and move nimbly between trees."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6944"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6953",
        "text": "Toucans are native to the Neotropical region, ranging from southern Mexico through Central America to northern Argentina and Bolivia. They inhabit tropical and subtropical forests at elevations up to 1,800 m. Some species are endangered due to habitat loss and the illegal pet trade."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6953"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6967",
        "text": "Toucans are primarily frugivores, feeding on a wide variety of tropical fruits such as figs, berries, and palm fruits. They also supplement their diet with insects, small lizards, eggs, and nestlings of other birds, making them opportunistic omnivores."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6967"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6972",
        "text": "Toucans nest in tree cavities, often using holes made by woodpeckers. Both parents share incubation duties for 15–18 days."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6972"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6971",
        "text": "NESTING"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6976",
        "text": "Toucans are social birds that live in small flocks of 6–20 individuals. They communicate using loud, frog-like croaking calls that echo through the forest."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6976"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6975",
        "text": "BEHAVIOR"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6980",
        "text": "Toucans play a vital ecological role as seed dispersers. They swallow fruit whole and excrete seeds far from the parent tree, helping regenerate tropical forests."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6980"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_fill_color",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6980"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6979",
        "text": "ECOLOGY"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6979"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6983",
        "text": "• A toucan's bill can be up to 20 cm long — nearly half its total body length of ~55 cm.\n\n• Toucans sleep by tucking their bill under their wing and folding their tail over their back to conserve space.\n\n• The Toco Toucan (Ramphastos toco) is the largest toucan species, weighing up to 680 grams."
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6983"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_content",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6987",
        "text": "SOURCES: National Geographic (nationalgeographic.com/animals/birds/facts/toucan) | Cornell Lab of Ornithology — Birds of the World (birdsoftheworld.org) | IUCN Red List (iucnredlist.org) | Smithsonian's National Zoo (nationalzoo.si.edu)"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "set_text_properties",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6987"
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "resize_node",
      "success": true,
      "result": {
        "id": "46:6987",
        "width": 1800,
        "height": 12
      }
    }
  ],
  "total": 30,
  "succeeded": 30
}

